hi everyone and welcome to biology  professor and today we're talking about  the four different kinds of  hypersensitivities so first what is a  hypersensitivity well a hypersensitivity  is an undesirable or an actually harmful  reaction of a person's immune system  sometimes also called an intolerance  specifically when we're talking about  sort of more undesirable things rather  than harmful things but but an  intolerance can be used to discuss all  of them so now let's talk about those  four different types  starting with type 1 type 1  hypersensitivity are when a specific  antibody specifically IgE recognizes  soluble antigen remember here that I G  stands for immunoglobulin which is  another name for antibody IgE is one of  the major types of antibodies so IgE  recognizes soluble antigen what does  this mean an antigen is just the thing  being recognized by the immune system in  this case it is something soluble so  it's not something that is cellular or  at the tissue level it's recognizing  just something that's kind of dissolved  and floating around in the body in  particular with type 1 hypersensitivity  IgE is recognizing things like pet  dander mold pollen things like that that  we think of as being related to seasonal  allergies and so IDE binds to that  soluble antigen when it finds it this  triggers an immune system cells called  mast cells to do something called 2d  granulate so the mast cells D granulate  and all that means is they basically  just fill out their guts these mast  cells are full sort of signaling  compounds that are then just released  into the body and the major one here  that we're concerned with is something  called histamine  you might recognize that word because it  is anti histamines that we take when we  want to relieve allergy symptoms and  that's because histamine release causes  the allergy symptoms that we associate  with a lot of these seasonal allergies  are pet allergies so I'm talking about  coughing sneezing red eyes watery eyes  hives those are all being caused by this  mask of histamine release and the body  and antihistamines can help to control  those symptoms so that is type 1  hypersensitivity now let's talk about  type 2 type 2 is also mediated by  antibodies but by different kinds of  antibodies rather than IgE the  antibodies that are in these type 2  hypersensitivity are IgG and IgM and  they recognize rather than the soluble  antigen stuff just happening to be  floating through the body they're  recognizing cellular antigens so  antigens that are actually on the  surface of the host cells and what this  does is when these antibodies recognize  and bind to cellular antigens they're  essentially biting to cells and causing  a lot of damage to those cells this can  be termed Saito Saito Purcell toxicity  so cytotoxicity is when cells are being  damaged some examples of type 2  hypersensitivity  are the recognition of certain cells  antigens self antigens means something  that is self something that belongs to  the host that is not a foreign entity  and this results in conditions known as  autoimmune disorders or autoimmunity  so some of these type 2 hypersensitivity  where you have IgG and IgM are you guys  any cellular antigens and caused some  damage to the cells this can be sort of  self cells and it can also in particular  the red blood cells so the [ __ ] groups if  you've heard like type a Type B type O 2  ad that has to do with carbohydrates  that are on the surface of red blood  cells and also if you've heard about  being like a positive or negative that a  positive and that negative is referring  to RH antigens which are also just  carbohydrates that are um stuck on to  red blood cells and so what can actually  happen here is if the immune system if  these antibodies recognize either the  [ __ ] or the RH antigens on red blood  cells as being floor and they start to  attack red blood cells and this is what  can be dangerous with blood transfusions  so hemolytic blood transfusions when  someone gets a transfusion of blood that  is not sort of compatible with their  blood type that is because these  antibodies are attacking these red blood  cells that are being put into the body  from a foreign source that aren't  compatible also you may have heard of  hemolytic disease of the newborn this is  when a mother's immune system actually  starts to attack the baby's blood when  the baby is sort of still in in utero or  just afterwards because of this sort of  incompatibility where the mom's  antibodies start to attack attack on  recognizing the baby's  different antigens now let's talk about  type 3 hypersensitivity type 3  hypersensitivity are specifically  characterized by these things called  immune complexes immune complexes are  when you have aggregations of antibody  and soluble antigen so not just one  antibody recognizing one soluble antigen  but multiple antibiotics  recognizing more than one soluble  antigen at a time buying into more than  one at a time and then those same  soluble antigens will be bound by other  antibodies and you just get this large  mass of all of these soluble antigens  that are being recognized by all of  these antibodies at once and that's  called an immune complex and the immune  complex will deposit into blood vessel  walls and once it's deposited into the  blood vessel walls it causes a couple of  things that happen first of all it  causes complement Cascades complement  are types of proteins that are part of  the immune system that cause this  cascade where all these complement  proteins are activating other complement  proteins and they eventually lead to  something called a membrane attack  complex where they form a hole in a cell  and bust it open and kill it and when  this is happening this is happening in  the body to host cells rather than to  pathogens when complement cascade  results in the death of pathogen so it's  a good thing when they result in the  death of host cells like in type 3  hypersensitivity that's a bad thing  something else that happened to meet  immune complexes deposit into blood  vessel walls is neutrophil degranulation  we had previously mentioned and type 1  hypersensitivity x' with mast cells need  granulating but that means they just  still forth and sort of secrete all  there all of these components that are  held inside themselves and for  neutrophils this means a lot of sort of  cytotoxic enzymes a lot of enzymes that  are meant to degrade and break down  things and so my complement starts to  have its cascade and neutrophils d  granulate there's all of the sort of  damage that's being done and this  results in widespread inflammation in  the body so lots of information lots of  tissue damage an example of this is the  autoimmune disorder lupus which you  might have heard of before and the lupus  is where antibodies bind either to self  DNA or to self proteins remember when we  have self antigens  like antibodies people that autoimmunity  so this is another place where  autoimmune disorders happen specifically  being caused by these immune complexes  and causing the sort of systemic  diseases like lupus now let's move on to  the final type of hypersensitivity type  4 type 4 is different than the first  three types in particular because it is  cell mediated it is actually regulated  by t-cells  so whereas types one and two and three  all involved antibodies doing something  that was going to harm the host type  four it's about t-cells doing something  wrong so the T cells can be th one or  th2 cells four different kinds of T  helper cells can also be cytotoxic  lymphocytes it's a different kind of T  cell but either way it's considered cell  mediated rather than antibody mediated  and it's also known as a delayed type of  hypersensitivity so delayed  hypersensitivity and all this means is  that the T cells they start to attack  something that otherwise shouldn't be  harmful in order to get it out of the  body this results and things like latex  allergies poison ivy the rash the  dermatitis that you get from contact  with poison ivy  it also transplant rejection so if you  hear about someone who maybe got a lung  transplant and then their body rejected  it that is because of a type 4  hypersensitivity where the T cells  recognized either the latex or the  poison ivy or the the lung transplant  one of these things and said hey that  shouldn't be here and they have this  really strong sort of overreaction to  eliminate what they do as a threat and  it results in either sort of an allergy  or a rash or at worst a major rejection  of the needle  organ transplant and known as a delayed  hypersensitivity because it can take  several days sometimes for these things  to for the symptoms to appear so several  days following following explosion  exposure in some cases and so that is  sort of it for the four types of  hypersensitivity I have another video  specifically on on antibody on antibody  structure and function and so if you're  interested in learning more about some  of these antibodies what they look like  what they do and then please check out  that video and thanks for watching  biology professor
 

